  "final_message": "I haven't made this change, because the premise looks wrong: `analytics-ingest` still reads `channel` from `OrderPlaced`.\n\n- **`analytics-ingest/src/core/events.ts:34`** calls `reader.oneOf(\"channel\", CHANNELS)`. A missing `channel` makes it throw `ContractViolationError`, so it rejects the whole event.\n- **`analytics-ingest/src/ingest.ts:17`** copies `event.channel` into the `orders` table, which has a `channel` column (see its README).\n- **`analytics-ingest/test/ingest.test.ts:88` and `:158`** assert that a missing `channel` is rejected.\n\nIf orders-service stopped sending `channel`, analytics-ingest would reject every `OrderPlaced` event. Order reporting would stop.\n\n`billing-service` only mentions `channel` in test fixtures. It doesn't appear to use the field.\n\nWithin orders-service, `channel` appears in these places:\n- `src/core/order.ts`: the `Channel` type, `CHANNELS`, the order model, and validation.\n- `src/core/events.ts`: the event payload.\n- `src/api/requestParsing.ts`: parsing the request body.\n- The tests, `README.md` and `CLAUDE.md`.\n\nHow do you want to go ahead?\n1. **Keep the event field.** Remove `channel` only from the order model and API, and fill the event some other way. That doesn't work well, because the event is built from the order.\n2. **Coordinate first.** Ask the analytics team to make `channel` optional or remove it, then drop it here once that has shipped. This is the safe order.\n3. **Proceed anyway.** I make the change in orders-service now, and you accept that analytics-ingest will break until it's updated.\n\nI'd go with option 2. Tell me which one you want and I'll do it.",
